1
00:00:01,386 --> 00:00:04,396
♫ In the not too distant future

2
00:00:04,396 --> 00:00:07,031
♫ Next Sunday, A.D.

3
00:00:07,031 --> 00:00:10,555
♫ There was a guy named Joel

4
00:00:10,555 --> 00:00:13,986
♫ Not to different from you or me

5
00:00:13,986 --> 00:00:16,879
♫ He worked at Gizmonic Institute

6
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:20,320
♫ Just another face in a red jumpsuit

7
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:23,198
♫ He did a good job cleaning up the place

8
00:00:23,198 --> 00:00:27,365
♫ But his bosses didn't like
him so they shot him into space

9
00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:32,325
♫ We'll send him cheesy movies

10
00:00:32,325 --> 00:00:35,812
♫ The worst we can find, la la la

11
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:38,787
♫ He'll have to sit and watch them all

12
00:00:38,787 --> 00:00:41,927
♫ And we'll monitor his mind, la la la

13
00:00:41,927 --> 00:00:44,611
♫ Now keep in mind Joel can't control

14
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:48,165
♫ Where the movies begin or end, la la la

15
00:00:48,165 --> 00:00:50,942
♫ Because he used those special parts

16
00:00:50,942 --> 00:00:54,609
♫ To make his robot friends

17
00:00:56,547 --> 00:00:57,971
♫ Robot Roll Call

18
00:00:57,971 --> 00:00:59,052
♫ Cambot, pan left

19
00:00:59,052 --> 00:01:00,448
♫ Gypsy, hi, girl

20
00:01:00,448 --> 00:01:01,908
♫ Tom Servo, What a cool guy

21
00:01:01,908 --> 00:01:04,029
♫ Crooooow, oh, wisecracker

22
00:01:04,029 --> 00:01:06,554
♫ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes

23
00:01:06,554 --> 00:01:09,806
♫ And other science facts, la la la

24
00:01:09,806 --> 00:01:12,611
♫ Then repeat to
yourself, it's just a show

25
00:01:12,611 --> 00:01:16,431
♫ I should really just relax

26
00:01:16,431 --> 00:01:20,598
♫ For Mystery Science Theater 3000 ♫

27
00:01:32,216 --> 00:01:33,618
- Alright, it's snack time!

28
00:01:33,618 --> 00:01:34,746
- Oh, hi everybody.

29
00:01:34,746 --> 00:01:37,227
Welcome to the Satellite
of Love, I'm Joel Robinson.

30
00:01:37,227 --> 00:01:39,142
We're just waiting for
this week's experiment.

31
00:01:39,142 --> 00:01:40,164
This is Crow T. Robot.

32
00:01:40,164 --> 00:01:41,005
- Boogie oogie.

33
00:01:41,005 --> 00:01:41,896
- And this is Tom Servo.

34
00:01:41,896 --> 00:01:43,230
- You got Bugles, I love Bugles.

35
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:45,671
- You know you guys, I was
just talking to TV's Frank

36
00:01:45,671 --> 00:01:47,464
and he was saying how this weeks movie

37
00:01:47,464 --> 00:01:48,557
is in black and white.

38
00:01:48,557 --> 00:01:49,447
Pretty cool, huh?

39
00:01:49,447 --> 00:01:51,382
- Oh no, not another black and white.

40
00:01:51,382 --> 00:01:52,636
- Oh, come on, Crow.

41
00:01:52,636 --> 00:01:54,087
Black and white's grittier.

42
00:01:54,087 --> 00:01:56,211
It helps get across the harsh realism

43
00:01:56,211 --> 00:01:57,795
that is every day life.

44
00:01:57,795 --> 00:01:59,192
- Oh gee, sounds like fun.

45
00:01:59,192 --> 00:02:00,669
The way I see it, Robinson,

46
00:02:00,669 --> 00:02:03,032
the Jones had a depressing
enough life as it was.

47
00:02:03,032 --> 00:02:05,273
And then, they filmed
it in black and white.

48
00:02:05,273 --> 00:02:07,689
Give me color, give me some pizzazz!

49
00:02:07,689 --> 00:02:09,047
- Hey, what do you think, Tom Servo?

50
00:02:09,047 --> 00:02:10,897
- Uh, about what?

51
00:02:10,897 --> 00:02:13,117
- Well, you know, that
how some of our movies

52
00:02:13,117 --> 00:02:15,574
are in black and white and some
of our movies are in color.

53
00:02:15,574 --> 00:02:18,222
- Uh, I'm not sure I
understand the question.

54
00:02:18,222 --> 00:02:19,367
- Oh, come on!

55
00:02:19,367 --> 00:02:20,651
Movie A, color.

56
00:02:20,651 --> 00:02:22,368
Movie B, black and white.

57
00:02:22,368 --> 00:02:24,422
- Look, we can't all
notice the same subtleties.

58
00:02:24,422 --> 00:02:25,562
- Subtleties?

59
00:02:25,562 --> 00:02:27,221
Why that's not even the point.

60
00:02:27,221 --> 00:02:28,549
- Just a second, Crow.

61
00:02:28,549 --> 00:02:29,382
Uhm, Tom?

62
00:02:30,645 --> 00:02:33,005
What color is my jumpsuit?

63
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:33,890
- Red.

64
00:02:33,890 --> 00:02:36,648
- Okay, and, what color is Crow?

65
00:02:36,648 --> 00:02:37,481
- Red.

66
00:02:37,481 --> 00:02:40,279
- [Voiceover] Commercials
in five, four, three, two--

67
00:02:40,279 --> 00:02:42,056
What color is Dr. Forester?

68
00:02:42,056 --> 00:02:43,668
- Red! What about it?

69
00:02:43,668 --> 00:02:45,253
- Okay, I think I know what's wrong.

70
00:02:45,253 --> 00:02:46,920
We'll be right back.

71
00:02:52,579 --> 00:02:54,487
- You have a very kind face.

72
00:02:54,487 --> 00:02:56,616
- Tom, now cut it out!

73
00:02:56,616 --> 00:02:59,555
I said you were colorblind, not blind.

74
00:02:59,555 --> 00:03:00,675
- I might as well be.

75
00:03:00,675 --> 00:03:03,916
For all the azure skies I've
missed, for the green fields,

76
00:03:03,916 --> 00:03:06,759
and apple blossoms I've never truly seen.

77
00:03:06,759 --> 00:03:09,168
- When I think of the
elaborate stories you've had to

78
00:03:09,168 --> 00:03:12,514
invent to cover for the
hideous lie you've been living.

79
00:03:12,514 --> 00:03:15,464
Oh, Tom, it wouldn't of mattered to me!

80
00:03:15,464 --> 00:03:16,843
- You guys, you guys.

81
00:03:16,843 --> 00:03:18,860
You guys, listen to me.

82
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:21,005
You're taking this all too seriously.

83
00:03:21,005 --> 00:03:24,917
I think Tom just has a simple
red-green color deficiency.

84
00:03:24,917 --> 00:03:27,879
Oh, uh, Joel, Bausch and Lomb are calling.

85
00:03:27,879 --> 00:03:30,173
- Oh, you're colorblind.

86
00:03:30,173 --> 00:03:32,191
Uh, what color is this?

87
00:03:32,191 --> 00:03:33,613
(chuckling)

88
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:34,896
What color is this?

89
00:03:34,896 --> 00:03:36,576
(evil chortle)

90
00:03:36,576 --> 00:03:37,409
Sorry.

91
00:03:38,643 --> 00:03:41,736
Well, Joel, you may not
know that Frank and I,

92
00:03:41,736 --> 00:03:44,059
with a little prompting
from the Utne Reader,

93
00:03:44,059 --> 00:03:46,525
have been hosting a
weekly discussion salon.

94
00:03:46,525 --> 00:03:48,476
- Yet with our hectic schedules,

95
00:03:48,476 --> 00:03:51,130
often times our guests will
arrive before we've had time

96
00:03:51,130 --> 00:03:54,551
to properly review
Susan Faludi's Backlash,

97
00:03:54,551 --> 00:03:57,724
much less give the apartment
that suitable bohemian look.

98
00:03:57,724 --> 00:03:59,594
- That's why we've developed these new,

99
00:03:59,594 --> 00:04:00,761
Beanbag Pants.

100
00:04:01,986 --> 00:04:04,866
Now, these pants allow
you to relax anywhere.

101
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:08,963
And to sit down and have
a nice hot cup of talk.

102
00:04:08,963 --> 00:04:11,624
Frank, why haven't you
put your pants on yet?

103
00:04:11,624 --> 00:04:12,989
(laughter)

104
00:04:12,989 --> 00:04:14,661
Joking, of course I kid--

105
00:04:14,661 --> 00:04:16,606
- Baby got back, uh!

106
00:04:16,606 --> 00:04:17,756
Actually, you know what?

107
00:04:17,756 --> 00:04:19,452
They're perfect for sit-ins.

108
00:04:19,452 --> 00:04:22,366
Imagine just once integrating
a Southern lunch counter

109
00:04:22,366 --> 00:04:24,251
in cushy comfort.

110
00:04:24,251 --> 00:04:25,217
- Yes, indeed.

111
00:04:25,217 --> 00:04:26,881
And they're perfect for
those whispery discussions

112
00:04:26,881 --> 00:04:29,190
of white-guilt with good friends.

113
00:04:29,190 --> 00:04:30,023
Frank?

114
00:04:33,946 --> 00:04:37,311
- You know, I thought the
invention lacked a certain...

115
00:04:37,311 --> 00:04:38,783
je ne sais quoi.

116
00:04:38,783 --> 00:04:40,347
A certain depth.

117
00:04:40,347 --> 00:04:43,008
- I disagree, and yet it's so refreshing

118
00:04:43,008 --> 00:04:45,878
to meet someone with a
fresh opinion like yours.

119
00:04:45,878 --> 00:04:49,024
- Gosh, sirs, that actually
seems like a really nice idea.

120
00:04:49,024 --> 00:04:51,996
Promoting open dialogue and social change.

121
00:04:51,996 --> 00:04:54,652
Today's invention exchange
from the Satellite of Love

122
00:04:54,652 --> 00:04:56,702
is based on the premise
that it's a really good idea

123
00:04:56,702 --> 00:04:59,103
to wear recycled paper as clothing.

124
00:04:59,103 --> 00:05:03,070
And we've got a persuasive
presentation to prove it.

125
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:05,247
- I'm Gypsy, and yesterdays news

126
00:05:05,247 --> 00:05:07,634
is tomorrow's fish and chip paper.

127
00:05:07,634 --> 00:05:08,972
Remember to pitch in.

128
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:12,056
- That's right Gypsy,
her dress was a tree,

129
00:05:12,056 --> 00:05:13,705
then it was a pile of newspapers,

130
00:05:13,705 --> 00:05:17,209
now it's a dress and soon it
will be a pithy greeting card.

131
00:05:17,209 --> 00:05:18,978
Pretty responsible, aye?

132
00:05:18,978 --> 00:05:21,186
- I'm Crow, and my suit is made entirely

133
00:05:21,186 --> 00:05:23,537
of used Viva Paper Towels.

134
00:05:23,537 --> 00:05:24,799
I wear it at the discos,

135
00:05:24,799 --> 00:05:26,104
'cause I'm the quicker picker upper.

136
00:05:26,104 --> 00:05:28,371
- Ha ha ha, true, Crow.

137
00:05:28,371 --> 00:05:29,769
It's so absorbent.

138
00:05:29,769 --> 00:05:31,759
You know you could spill soup, drool,

139
00:05:31,759 --> 00:05:34,099
or even drop a pitcher
of margaritas on him

140
00:05:34,099 --> 00:05:36,868
and he'll remain warm and dry.

141
00:05:36,868 --> 00:05:38,839
- And I'm Tom Servo,
and I may be colorblind,

142
00:05:38,839 --> 00:05:40,547
but it's plain to see I'm wearing a dress.

143
00:05:40,547 --> 00:05:41,704
What am I, that girl?

144
00:05:41,704 --> 00:05:44,782
- Maybe so, in that case
can't afford to be seen

145
00:05:44,782 --> 00:05:46,337
in the same thing twice.

146
00:05:46,337 --> 00:05:49,354
Change in a flash with
our flash-paper clothing.

147
00:05:49,354 --> 00:05:51,687
Just one flick of the BIC and...

148
00:05:51,687 --> 00:05:52,520
- Uh oh.

149
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:54,413
Wah-ho, ow oh oh.

150
00:05:54,413 --> 00:05:56,141
Phew, oh for crying out loud.

151
00:05:56,141 --> 00:05:58,889
Not only am I colorblind,
I'm flash-blind now too.

152
00:05:58,889 --> 00:06:01,165
Oh, jeeze, I'm gonna go lay down.

153
00:06:01,165 --> 00:06:02,824
Whoa, owchie!

154
00:06:02,824 --> 00:06:03,657
- There go the drapes.

155
00:06:03,657 --> 00:06:04,991
- What do you think sirs?

156
00:06:04,991 --> 00:06:06,391
- Sorry, we missed that, Joel.

157
00:06:06,391 --> 00:06:10,392
Anyway, your movie this week
is called Manhunt in Space.

158
00:06:10,392 --> 00:06:14,080
But first, a little
kinescope suppository from

159
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,402
our video depository.

160
00:06:16,402 --> 00:06:18,902
It's an old, General Hospital.

161
00:06:20,112 --> 00:06:21,183
(alarm sounding)

162
00:06:21,183 --> 00:06:24,933
(all talking simultaneously)

163
00:06:32,550 --> 00:06:36,867
- [Tom] Oh, don't make me walk
all the way over there, Phil.

164
00:06:36,867 --> 00:06:38,184
- If this will cheer you up any,

165
00:06:38,184 --> 00:06:39,949
I called Cynthia and asked her to dinner,

166
00:06:39,949 --> 00:06:41,825
she accepted, celebrate the engagement.

167
00:06:41,825 --> 00:06:43,662
- [Crow] Thanks nurse-ratchet.

168
00:06:43,662 --> 00:06:44,912
- That's great.

169
00:06:45,774 --> 00:06:46,999
- Well, wait it was your idea--

170
00:06:46,999 --> 00:06:48,931
- Alright, alright, I said it was great.

171
00:06:48,931 --> 00:06:50,248
Didn't I?

172
00:06:50,248 --> 00:06:51,515
We'll have a ball.

173
00:06:51,515 --> 00:06:53,259
- [Tom] Really cheered you up, aye Phil?

174
00:06:53,259 --> 00:06:54,699
- [Crow] Jeepers.

175
00:06:54,699 --> 00:06:55,575
- [Joel] Sorry, dear.

176
00:06:55,575 --> 00:06:56,854
- [Crow] I will now express a modern dance

177
00:06:56,854 --> 00:06:59,041
to express my true feelings.

178
00:06:59,041 --> 00:07:01,101
- [Joel] Oh, this is about
general hospital stuff.

179
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:03,032
You know, those little
wrist tags, billing,

180
00:07:03,032 --> 00:07:04,514
that kind of thing.

181
00:07:04,514 --> 00:07:05,698
- [Voiceover] General
Hospital is brought to you by,

182
00:07:05,698 --> 00:07:06,531
Kennel--

183
00:07:06,531 --> 00:07:09,472
(all three barking)

184
00:07:09,472 --> 00:07:11,889
- [Crow] Going up, Mr. Tyler?

185
00:07:14,218 --> 00:07:15,409
Uh, excuse me.

186
00:07:15,409 --> 00:07:17,385
Uh, let me get, excuse me, sorry, hello.

187
00:07:17,385 --> 00:07:20,715
- [Joel] Sounds like
General Hospital on ice.

188
00:07:20,715 --> 00:07:21,769
- Good morning, Lucy.

189
00:07:21,769 --> 00:07:22,924
- Good morning, Jessie.

190
00:07:22,924 --> 00:07:23,757
(buzzing)
- I'll get it.

191
00:07:23,757 --> 00:07:24,864
- [Tom] She's the dark nurse of the soul.

192
00:07:24,864 --> 00:07:25,762
- [Joel] Mm mm.

193
00:07:25,762 --> 00:07:27,047
- Yes, Dr. Hardy

194
00:07:27,047 --> 00:07:29,225
The new x-rays on Mrs. Lions?

195
00:07:29,225 --> 00:07:30,754
Well, I wouldn't know,
I just came on duty.

196
00:07:30,754 --> 00:07:32,264
- [Crow] I think she's
been into the percodan.

197
00:07:32,264 --> 00:07:33,431
- [Tom] G'yah.

198
00:07:35,528 --> 00:07:36,591
- Lucille?

199
00:07:36,591 --> 00:07:37,424
- Hmm?

200
00:07:37,424 --> 00:07:38,426
- The tilt pillar on Mrs. Lions,

201
00:07:38,426 --> 00:07:39,268
has it come up from x-ray?

202
00:07:39,268 --> 00:07:40,101
- Oh yes.

203
00:07:41,648 --> 00:07:42,767
- [Joel] Here are your head-shots for the

204
00:07:42,767 --> 00:07:45,434
Young and the Restless audition.

205
00:07:46,838 --> 00:07:48,074
- [Tom] It's a new cot!

206
00:07:48,074 --> 00:07:49,383
Oh.

207
00:07:49,383 --> 00:07:51,482
- [Crow] Hello, I've been in
the bell tower all morning.

208
00:07:51,482 --> 00:07:52,658
- [Lucille] Good morning, Dr. Lewis.

209
00:07:52,658 --> 00:07:53,491
- Good morning, Lucille.

210
00:07:53,491 --> 00:07:54,674
Is Jessie in?

211
00:07:54,674 --> 00:07:56,080
- Yes, she just came on duty.

212
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:57,857
- [Tom] Yeah, she's a shade bit broody.

213
00:07:57,857 --> 00:07:59,706
- Didn't you come to
the hospital together?

214
00:07:59,706 --> 00:08:02,605
- No, I left the apartment before she did.

215
00:08:02,605 --> 00:08:03,605
Took a walk.

216
00:08:04,497 --> 00:08:05,330
- [Crow] See what you can do

217
00:08:05,330 --> 00:08:07,074
about making this lobby spookier.

218
00:08:07,074 --> 00:08:11,241
- [Joel] Jeeze, he really
sucked the air out of this room.

219
00:08:12,116 --> 00:08:13,133
- Alright.

220
00:08:13,133 --> 00:08:16,036
I guess it's a wonderful
but weird institution.

221
00:08:16,036 --> 00:08:16,925
Aye, Clampett?

222
00:08:16,925 --> 00:08:18,344
- I wouldn't know Ms. March.

223
00:08:18,344 --> 00:08:19,365
- [Crow] I'm not very bright.

224
00:08:19,365 --> 00:08:21,325
- Looking at it from the
other end of the stethoscope,

225
00:08:21,325 --> 00:08:22,158
neither would I.

226
00:08:22,158 --> 00:08:23,315
- [Tom] Ah, Swiss Miss,

227
00:08:23,315 --> 00:08:26,398
will you get me another cup of cocoa?

228
00:08:28,143 --> 00:08:32,310
- [Crow] More letters on how
unappealing you are, sir.

229
00:08:33,228 --> 00:08:34,924
- [Joel] She kinda
looks like Uncle Miltie.

230
00:08:34,924 --> 00:08:36,007
- [Tom] Yeah.

231
00:08:37,344 --> 00:08:40,532
- [Crow] Huh, here comes nurse-feratu.

232
00:08:40,532 --> 00:08:41,699
- [Tom] Yeesh.

233
00:08:44,237 --> 00:08:45,645
Well I think everybody
admitted to this hospital

234
00:08:45,645 --> 00:08:46,978
commits suicide.

235
00:08:48,958 --> 00:08:49,900
- Jessie?

236
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:50,960
- [All Three] Ee-yah!

237
00:08:50,960 --> 00:08:51,970
- Did you decide to invite--

238
00:08:51,970 --> 00:08:53,242
- [Crow] My God, you're ugly.

239
00:08:53,242 --> 00:08:54,930
- To celebrate the engagement?

240
00:08:54,930 --> 00:08:57,287
- [Tom] Did you ih-vite
Cyn-phia and K-eh-en Mah-tan

241
00:08:57,287 --> 00:08:59,105
tuh deen-ah tuh cel-uh-ba-rate
yur ehn-gah-hage-mant.

242
00:08:59,105 --> 00:09:00,134
- It'll help clear the air.

243
00:09:00,134 --> 00:09:01,047
- [Joel] Pshhh.

244
00:09:01,047 --> 00:09:02,036
- Oh, I don't know Lucille,

245
00:09:02,036 --> 00:09:03,288
I'm having my doubts that it's very smart.

246
00:09:03,288 --> 00:09:06,658
- Well it's what Phil suggested, isn't it?

247
00:09:06,658 --> 00:09:07,514
- [Tom] Phill-th?

248
00:09:07,514 --> 00:09:08,571
- It's what Phil suggested,

249
00:09:08,571 --> 00:09:09,927
but I'm not very sure he really wants to.

250
00:09:09,927 --> 00:09:11,078
- [Crow] Oh Phil this, Phil that.

251
00:09:11,078 --> 00:09:12,539
Everything's Phil, Phil, Phil.

252
00:09:12,539 --> 00:09:13,412
- What good is it gonna do?

253
00:09:13,412 --> 00:09:15,775
- Well it will sort of make
the engagement official.

254
00:09:15,775 --> 00:09:16,943
- [Tom] Whe-ell itch-ill short-off make-sh

255
00:09:16,943 --> 00:09:17,943
the en-gah-hage-ment off-fish-ill.

256
00:09:17,943 --> 00:09:19,432
- That Cynthia is going
to marry Ken Martin.

257
00:09:19,432 --> 00:09:20,425
That's what it'll do.

258
00:09:20,425 --> 00:09:21,455
- [Tom] Tatch what it'll dooo.

259
00:09:21,455 --> 00:09:23,078
- At least, I hope you're right.

260
00:09:23,078 --> 00:09:26,828
- [Joel] Would you please
take a breath mint?

261
00:09:28,127 --> 00:09:30,257
- [Tom] So, uh, what's
the barber doing here?

262
00:09:30,257 --> 00:09:32,085
- Oh I'd like to
counter-sign the medication

263
00:09:32,085 --> 00:09:33,011
for Mr. Turner.

264
00:09:33,011 --> 00:09:33,920
- Oh, yeah.

265
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:37,003
- [Crow] Jeeze, he was old even then.

266
00:09:42,868 --> 00:09:45,360
- Oh, uh, I'll be in Martha Lions' room.

267
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:47,020
- Yes, Dr. Hardy.

268
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:48,413
- Good morning, Jessie.

269
00:09:48,413 --> 00:09:49,469
- Morning, Dr. Hardy.

270
00:09:49,469 --> 00:09:52,172
- [Joel] Man I've still got it, don't I?

271
00:09:52,172 --> 00:09:53,025
Damn I'm good.

272
00:09:53,025 --> 00:09:57,217
"Good morning, Jessie"
was a stroke of genius.

273
00:09:57,217 --> 00:09:58,774
- [Crow] Oh doctor! Do you mind?

274
00:09:58,774 --> 00:10:03,083
- [Woman] I think you're
being a little unfair.

275
00:10:03,083 --> 00:10:04,666
- [Tom] She's nude.

276
00:10:05,621 --> 00:10:08,161
- And maybe it is a simple
case of indigestion,

277
00:10:08,161 --> 00:10:09,594
but I'm worried.

278
00:10:09,594 --> 00:10:13,123
I want to be absolutely sure
that there's nothing wrong.

279
00:10:13,123 --> 00:10:14,861
- [Crow] Oh, my barber's here. Gotta go.

280
00:10:14,861 --> 00:10:17,071
- George, Dr. Hardy
just came into the room.

281
00:10:17,071 --> 00:10:20,221
He looks as though he might
have some news for me?

282
00:10:20,221 --> 00:10:22,337
- [Crow] Oh, it's just about
that cyst with teeth and hair.

283
00:10:22,337 --> 00:10:23,651
- Yes, indeed he does.

284
00:10:23,651 --> 00:10:26,568
Yes, I'll call you back, dear. Bye.

285
00:10:28,236 --> 00:10:29,560
- [Joel] Hi, that was Vince Edwards,

286
00:10:29,560 --> 00:10:30,873
he gave me a second opinion.

287
00:10:30,873 --> 00:10:32,270
- Give us a moment.

288
00:10:32,270 --> 00:10:34,465
- There is something wrong with me?

289
00:10:34,465 --> 00:10:37,632
- [Tom] Yes, you've been hospitalized.

290
00:10:40,048 --> 00:10:43,724
- You have what's known
as a hiatus hernia.

291
00:10:43,724 --> 00:10:44,902
- [Crow] Which means I win the pool,

292
00:10:44,902 --> 00:10:46,000
but that's beside the point.

293
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:46,984
- What is that?

294
00:10:46,984 --> 00:10:48,654
- Well, it's nothing to
be too alarmed about.

295
00:10:48,654 --> 00:10:50,352
But it is to be taken seriously.

296
00:10:50,352 --> 00:10:51,628
And it's quite rare.

297
00:10:51,628 --> 00:10:53,891
- [Tom] Nothing an expensive
operation can't complicate.

298
00:10:53,891 --> 00:10:55,663
- How were you finally
able to diagnose that?

299
00:10:55,663 --> 00:10:56,741
- Those, uh, last x-rays.

300
00:10:56,741 --> 00:10:59,011
We took the tilt x-rays, they revealed it.

301
00:10:59,011 --> 00:10:59,844
- [Tom] And you might of noticed,

302
00:10:59,844 --> 00:11:01,255
you've been vomiting a lot lately.

303
00:11:01,255 --> 00:11:02,088
- What is it?

304
00:11:02,088 --> 00:11:03,121
I've never heard of it.

305
00:11:03,121 --> 00:11:04,526
Hiatus hernia?

306
00:11:04,526 --> 00:11:05,428
- [Tom] You calling me a liar?

307
00:11:05,428 --> 00:11:07,595
- [Crow] Get out! Get out!

308
00:11:09,185 --> 00:11:10,029
- Well--

309
00:11:10,029 --> 00:11:11,200
- [Tom] The body sees a hernia

310
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:12,768
as a series of ones and zeros.

311
00:11:12,768 --> 00:11:15,777
- Point at where the esophagus
goes into the stomach.

312
00:11:15,777 --> 00:11:18,599
The constriction is most likely
to happen when the stomach

313
00:11:18,599 --> 00:11:21,873
is full, after you've had
something to eat or drink.

314
00:11:21,873 --> 00:11:23,491
- [Joel] Uh, is this
gonna be on the final?

315
00:11:23,491 --> 00:11:25,160
- Put simply, when the stomach enlarges,

316
00:11:25,160 --> 00:11:27,969
it gets pinched at this opening.

317
00:11:27,969 --> 00:11:30,523
- [Tom] To put it even more
simply, you eat like a pig.

318
00:11:30,523 --> 00:11:32,150
- After all my doubts.

319
00:11:32,150 --> 00:11:33,407
- [Tom] I'm Eric Stoltz, in Mask.

320
00:11:33,407 --> 00:11:35,715
- And all of George's insisting there

321
00:11:35,715 --> 00:11:37,165
was nothing wrong with me.

322
00:11:37,165 --> 00:11:38,884
- [Crow] That bastard.

323
00:11:38,884 --> 00:11:40,496
- There is something wrong with me.

324
00:11:40,496 --> 00:11:43,618
- [Tom] Yes, you're a
spooky New York actress.

325
00:11:43,618 --> 00:11:46,962
- Yes, I was pretty sure
your pain wasn't imaginary.

326
00:11:46,962 --> 00:11:50,037
In fact, the pain associated
with a hiatus hernia,

327
00:11:50,037 --> 00:11:52,800
is very intense and it's almost identical

328
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:55,189
to coronary inclusion or heart attack.

329
00:11:55,189 --> 00:11:56,022
- [Crow] Here, let me punch you

330
00:11:56,022 --> 00:11:57,239
in the sternum to simulate the pain.

331
00:11:57,239 --> 00:11:58,953
- Why does the pain go
away after a few minutes?

332
00:11:58,953 --> 00:12:00,970
- Well, it usually disappears when you

333
00:12:00,970 --> 00:12:02,611
get up and walk around a bit.

334
00:12:02,611 --> 00:12:03,650
- [Tom] Hint. Hint.

335
00:12:03,650 --> 00:12:04,827
- [Dr. Hardy] Well, then, you see

336
00:12:04,827 --> 00:12:06,534
the stomach has more room.

337
00:12:06,534 --> 00:12:09,418
- [Joel] Well I could
see how that would--Huh?

338
00:12:09,418 --> 00:12:12,027
- Steve, can something be done for this?

339
00:12:12,027 --> 00:12:12,860
- [Tom] Nope.

340
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:13,693
- Of course.

341
00:12:13,693 --> 00:12:14,794
- [Crow] (snickering)

342
00:12:14,794 --> 00:12:16,806
- that you try a couple
of weeks of hospital care

343
00:12:16,806 --> 00:12:19,420
with special attention to diet.

344
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:22,441
- [Crow] And then I'll do
my Gregory Peck impression.

345
00:12:22,441 --> 00:12:23,758
- If we can't cure the condition that way,

346
00:12:23,758 --> 00:12:24,702
or come close to it,

347
00:12:24,702 --> 00:12:26,774
we may have to consider surgery.

348
00:12:26,774 --> 00:12:29,805
Now let me assure you, Martha.
- [Martha] Surgery?

349
00:12:29,805 --> 00:12:31,254
There's no great cause for alarm.

350
00:12:31,254 --> 00:12:33,232
- [Tom] Oh it's dangerous
and painful, but don't worry.

351
00:12:33,232 --> 00:12:35,559
- And I'm very hopeful
that we can get rid of it

352
00:12:35,559 --> 00:12:36,874
without surgery.

353
00:12:36,874 --> 00:12:38,642
- [Joel] Though I have
performed surgery once before,

354
00:12:38,642 --> 00:12:40,652
although the person didn't
survive, I feel confident.

355
00:12:40,652 --> 00:12:42,648
- Who's going to tell my
husband all about this?

356
00:12:42,648 --> 00:12:44,558
- [Tom] How about his wife?

357
00:12:44,558 --> 00:12:45,696
- I will if you like.

358
00:12:45,696 --> 00:12:47,743
But I think it might be a better idea

359
00:12:47,743 --> 00:12:49,708
if you tell Dr. Lions yourself.

360
00:12:49,708 --> 00:12:50,797
- [Tom] Maybe you could use a puppet.

361
00:12:50,797 --> 00:12:51,948
- [Dr. Hardy] Of course,
I'll be happy to answer

362
00:12:51,948 --> 00:12:56,498
he might ask or have a
consultation with him about it.

363
00:12:56,498 --> 00:12:58,351
- George is going to be very upset.

364
00:12:58,351 --> 00:12:59,790
- [Joel] He hates hiatus hernias!

365
00:12:59,790 --> 00:13:04,220
- [Dr. Hardy] Because you
really have something wrong?

366
00:13:04,220 --> 00:13:05,640
- Not only that...

367
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:06,473
It's you.

368
00:13:07,745 --> 00:13:10,675
You proved that he was wrong.

369
00:13:10,675 --> 00:13:13,528
He said that it was indigestion
and just my imagination.

370
00:13:13,528 --> 00:13:14,578
- [Crow] Running away with her.

371
00:13:14,578 --> 00:13:18,201
- Hiatus hernia's a
difficult thing to diagnose,

372
00:13:18,201 --> 00:13:20,237
in fact it's very difficult.

373
00:13:20,237 --> 00:13:21,838
Well look at the trouble I had.

374
00:13:21,838 --> 00:13:22,675
- [Crow] And I'm brilliant!

375
00:13:22,675 --> 00:13:25,258
- Yes, but you did diagnose it.

376
00:13:28,843 --> 00:13:32,176
- [Crow] Okay, so your husband's a putz.

377
00:13:33,765 --> 00:13:35,343
- Anyway, for a couple
of weeks will give you

378
00:13:35,343 --> 00:13:37,208
plenty of rest and a special diet.

379
00:13:37,208 --> 00:13:40,702
So don't worry, we'll
take good care of you.

380
00:13:40,702 --> 00:13:42,340
- [Tom] Now, about that hair.

381
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:43,898
- Will you come in to see me?

382
00:13:43,898 --> 00:13:45,480
- [Tom] (smooching)

383
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:46,839
- You're my patient, remember?

384
00:13:46,839 --> 00:13:48,094
- [Crow] And don't forget we've scheduled

385
00:13:48,094 --> 00:13:51,511
that lobotomy first thing Monday morning.

386
00:13:53,473 --> 00:13:56,442
- [Tom] And now taking the ice,
your Minnesota North Stars.

387
00:13:56,442 --> 00:13:57,613
- [All] (quiet cheering)

388
00:13:57,613 --> 00:14:01,030
- [Crow] Bum bum bum bum bum bum bum bum.

389
00:14:13,102 --> 00:14:16,037
Wow, look at all the Juji Fruits.

390
00:14:16,037 --> 00:14:17,723
- [Joel] Well, I saw Howard Hunt in space.

391
00:14:17,723 --> 00:14:18,815
- [Tom] I saw Linda Hunt in space.

392
00:14:18,815 --> 00:14:20,660
- [Crow] I have saw Hunt's Hall in space.

393
00:14:20,660 --> 00:14:23,068
- [Joel] An adventure of tomorrow,

394
00:14:23,068 --> 00:14:25,318
for today's troubled teens.

395
00:14:27,235 --> 00:14:28,642
- [Crow] Tracy and
Hepburn, Bogart and Bacall,

396
00:14:28,642 --> 00:14:30,992
Mansfield and Lyden.

397
00:14:30,992 --> 00:14:33,242
- [Joel] Mama Maurice Cass.

398
00:14:36,656 --> 00:14:39,866
- [Tom] They say this really
is a L'estrange movie.

399
00:14:39,866 --> 00:14:41,193
- [Crow] Or a Strenge movie.

400
00:14:41,193 --> 00:14:44,298
- [Joel] Cough up a Roy Luby.

401
00:14:44,298 --> 00:14:48,465
♫ Oz never did give
nothing to Bill Tinsman ♫

402
00:14:52,088 --> 00:14:55,996
- [Crow] I wanted to Hoerl
after I read the screenplay.

403
00:14:55,996 --> 00:14:57,413
- [Joel] Hi, Guy.

404
00:14:59,989 --> 00:15:04,156
- [Tom] Arthur Pierson, the
inventor of the salted nut roll.

405
00:15:05,235 --> 00:15:07,200
- [Crow] Oh, is the
great Hollingsworth Morse

406
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,567
going to direct?

407
00:15:08,567 --> 00:15:09,650
- [All] Oooo.

408
00:15:12,164 --> 00:15:13,877
- [Joel] This is the part where the, uhm,

409
00:15:13,877 --> 00:15:17,544
zagnut is fertilized
by the salted nut roll.

410
00:15:19,069 --> 00:15:20,841
- [Tom] Look, it's the
MST3K logo up there--

411
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:21,994
- [Joel] You're not
supposed to know about...

412
00:15:21,994 --> 00:15:26,130
- [Tom] Oh (clears throat and whistles)

413
00:15:26,130 --> 00:15:27,249
- Vena.

414
00:15:27,249 --> 00:15:29,677
- [Crow] Oh ho Vena-ha.

415
00:15:29,677 --> 00:15:30,726
- Reggie!

416
00:15:30,726 --> 00:15:32,370
- [Joel] Bad dog! Bad dog.

417
00:15:32,370 --> 00:15:35,413
- You know I'm expecting
to hear from Rocky.

418
00:15:35,413 --> 00:15:37,389
- I wanted to bring in Casa 7.

419
00:15:37,389 --> 00:15:38,389
There it is.

420
00:15:39,235 --> 00:15:41,652
- [Crow] It's a crunch berry.

421
00:15:43,050 --> 00:15:43,883
- [Tom] Times up!

422
00:15:43,883 --> 00:15:45,795
- I contacted Casa's
central control station,

423
00:15:45,795 --> 00:15:48,176
and they're holding communications open.

424
00:15:48,176 --> 00:15:50,024
- Can I speak to my brother Paul?

425
00:15:50,024 --> 00:15:50,857
- Well I asked for him.

426
00:15:50,857 --> 00:15:52,361
They said he's at the
landing field inspecting

427
00:15:52,361 --> 00:15:54,858
